summary:
Teaching-track read of public/data/voiced-teachings.json notes[1500..2249]: 750 notes, 40 videos, 348 opening / 276 middlegame / 126 endgame, student White 444 / Black 306. The `concepts` field is empty on every note and `teaches` is filled on only 91, so the teaching sits almost entirely in `explains`. Rough keyword counts over that slice (they overlap): roughly 100 mention tactics or traps, ~89 name an opening or variation, ~83 reject an alternative ("not X", "rather than"), ~81 talk about opening or closing the position, ~69 are if-then consequence lines, ~69 explain a recapture choice, ~60 are about squares, holes or outposts, ~32 are about prevention, ~20 name a drawback, and 162 (about 22%) are bare move calls with no teaching.

The reference coach's core shape, which matches F18: state the move, name its PURPOSE (often two jobs), name the tempting or natural alternative and give the concrete reason it fails, give the consequence as "if they do X, you have Y", and close with a portable rule or habit. Openings are named, placed in history and reputation, compared with what most people at the student's level play, and every opponent deviation gets its punishment (the early f4 met by d5, the bishop-to-c5 sideline met by the centre fork trick, the Bg4 met by a sacrifice on f7). He makes the student THINK by asking the question before answering it ("what are you preparing with a3? ask it that way and the plan appears"; "does anybody see it?"; "how did I spot it? I looked at the drawback of their last move").

Most of these acts have a computer today. The densest are moveInsight.ts, speedRunReads.ts, deliberation.ts, moveIntent.ts, recaptureChoice.ts, ruleException.ts, refutedAlternative.ts, theoryDeparture.ts, openingAnnouncement.ts and forkTrick.ts. Clear MISSING items in this slice:
- the pin that breaks with tempo;
- who benefits when the centre opens or closes (the side with the space or square control wants it closed);
- the half-closed centre that shuts in an enemy fianchetto bishop, and a fianchetto bishop that has left home and cannot return;
- a knight's square count from the centre versus the rim (the "lords over eight squares" nugget);
- "prepare your ANSWER to their break" as the second kind of prophylaxis, beside preventing it;
- plan pacing: prepare slowly, strike fast, take small gains, finish development before running the plan;
- consistency: having made the commitment, follow through with its point;
- trading queens only when it speeds the mate, never when it dissolves the attack;
- giving up a bishop that is lost anyway for the most it can take (desperado);
- reversing a gambit.

Things in this slice the app must NOT copy: the coach admitting he is unsure, sloppy or wrong, playing on gut feel, and talk about the clock or the opponent's psychology (F18: the coach always knows the answer). Name drops, video self-reference and opponent-specific preparation are also out (F0d, and they teach nothing).

Rhythm: in the opening, nearly every move gets a reason, often several, plus the opening's name, its history and the side choices (V19). In the middlegame the coach stays terse until a decision point, then gives one big paragraph weighing candidates, the plan and the tactic (notes 1804, 1940, 2249). The endgame is mostly bare move calls, broken by technique nuggets (avoid stalemate, connected passers, deflecting the rook with one passer to queen the other). Notes 1639-1678 and 1806-1859 are pure move narration for whole games, which is exactly the "description, not teaching" the rulebook cuts. Our tape must not imitate those stretches. Everything above is paraphrased; the coach's name appears nowhere.

Slice read: notes 3750-4499 (750 notes, 50 videos; 365 opening / 265 middlegame / 120 endgame). Every computer citation below was checked by grep in /home/user/wt-work/src/services.

The reference coach teaches through about 26 repeating acts, and nearly all of them hang on one habit. He names the TEMPTING or NATURAL choice, says the concrete reason it fails, then gives the move that works. Examples: "not Bf5, which runs into Nh4"; "not the queen recapture, that loses a tempo"; "not e5, it drops a pawn". He almost never says a move without its point. In the opening he says WHY the line is theory: what the move prepares, what it stops, and what it allows. A small or quiet move gets its hidden job named, for example c6 exists so d4 can come with gain of time later, and a5 stops b4. He states consequences as a chain: "if X then Y, and that is why". He also gives the condition under which the idea works ("only because the bishop on c4 already covers the escape square"; "f6 is safe here because they have no light-squared bishop").

Golden nuggets in this slice:
- a pin is fatal when no pawn defends the pinned piece;
- defend with the least valuable piece;
- bad bishops defend good pawns;
- how far a long-range piece retreats says nothing about whether the move is good;
- whenever a pawn moves, look for the weaknesses it leaves;
- two pieces a knight's distance apart means a fork should light up;
- queen and king on one line means look for a pin;
- a piece defended only once is a target;
- telling temporary from permanent;
- the best-case-scenario test before a pawn storm;
- give the material back when unsure in a gambit.

Most acts already have a computer in src/services. The strongest matches are speedRunReads.ts (keepTension :49, provokes :125, overprotect :209, bestCasePlan :435, rejectedMoveLater :486, forceConcession :512, flexibleFirst :549), refutedAlternative.ts:81, deliberation.ts:144, moveInsight.ts (weakenedBy :523, doubleAttack :83, mechanismContrast :459), latentFork/forkTrick, pinGeometry/pinPressure, outpost, recaptureChoice, positionTransformation, ruleException, kingSafety and endgamePawnReads.

MISSING:
1. A "condition-it-depends-on" computer: why the same move is bad in one position and good in another.
2. A "trapped piece by pawn advance" live detector. The tag exists only in the classifiers and the puzzle tools; there is no live board computer.
3. "Induce a move you want": a check or attack whose real point is the forced reply that ruins the opponent's structure.
4. "Temporary vs permanent" weighing.
5. "Uncastled / stranded king as a standing target, with the plan built round it". detectCentralKingDanger (kingSafety.ts:142) covers part of this.
6. "Defended once = target" (overload by count). Partly covered in speedRunReads, but there is no named computer.
7. "Retreat is not passive" (long-range piece keeps its line).
8. "Least valuable defender" as a teaching fact. It exists only in the capture-order lines of thinkingExchangeChain.ts:191.
9. "Give the material back" in gambits.

Negative examples worth logging as anti-patterns for F01 (description, not teaching):
- Two stretches are pure play-by-play. Notes 4137-4188 (a whole game) and 4404-4454 read like "You develop the knight to c3."
- Some notes carry clock, ego and "I" chatter: 3915, 3925, 4068.
Neither must ever be copied.

Read notes[0..186] of public/data/voiced-teachings.json: 8 games, 187 notes (87 opening, 86 middlegame, 14 endgame; 102 on the student's move, 85 on the opponent's). Below, n# means the index into notes[]. I catalogued 70 teaching acts and mapped each one to src/services with file:line. Seven things the coarse census missed:

1) Computers that exist but are gated so the reference coach's own examples don't fire. tempoCount needs the student to have a development lead (tempoCount.ts:69); at n7 they had 1 minor out against 2, so it stays silent. opponentHabits needs at least 2 minors at home (thinkAloud.ts:187). ruleException's pawn-in-front-of-the-king exception needs a castled king, and its early-queen exception needs the queen to hit something (ruleException.ts:115, :124), which misses n47 and n121. moveTiming ignores costs under 2 points (moveTiming.ts:33). trappedOnBoard looks at rooks and queens only (reviewTeachingPoints.ts:812). bluffDetector skips pawn lunges (bluffDetector.ts:43). findXrays needs a friendly blocker and a more valuable target (positionReadingService.ts:851, :860). isRealPin rejects a piece shielding an equal piece (pinGeometry.ts:146). retreatKeepsBreak misses a pawn double-step through the vacated square (speedRunReads.ts:429). deliberation only weighs alternatives proven by a material or line loss (deliberation.ts:260, :278), and most of the reference coach's alternatives fail for positional reasons.

2) Computers that only work for one seat or one tense. noRetreat and heavyTiedDown can read either side but are called only on the student's own pieces (moveInsight.ts:287, :288). findConcession runs only on moves already played (backwardLook.ts:180, :305), never on the tempting candidate. refutedAlternative only covers the student's popular alternative (refutedAlternativeCore.ts:87). payoffFor closes a promise only on the student's own move, within 16 plies (learnBoardTeaching.ts:897, :917). The reference coach's callbacks fire on the opponent's move, and one comes 20 plies later (n38 to n55).

3) One computer gives a provably wrong plan. The closed-centre template (positionReadingService.ts:1087-1094) tells the student "you break on the queenside with c5". In game 4 after 11.Be3, both b6 and d6 attack c5 (checked with chess.js), and the reference coach teaches the opposite wing (n84-85).

4) Two facts are computed and then thrown away. walkForcedSequence (onlyMoveSequence.ts:57) has no production importer. structureSignature.lockedCenter (boardStructure.ts:303) has no reader. These are exactly the facts behind his "it holds to a perpetual" (n95) and "in a locked centre the move order stops mattering" (n79).

5) Teaching families with no computer at all in this slice:
- a flag for strategic decisions, plus weighing candidates by positional reasons;
- forward chains of consequences for a plan move;
- choosing the pawn structure, and choosing the wing;
- a teaser now with the reveal on the opponent's move;
- the common small slip at a position (between F04's trap bar and silence);
- king-hunt technique;
- "prefer the simpler win";
- "this is only safe because of that earlier move".

6) Defects in the corpus itself (D10/V1).
- 10 notes (n95, 100, 103, 106, 107, 109, 110, 111, 115, 139) speak in the first person or say "let's". They pass src/data/perspectiveVoice.test.ts: it bans only we/our/us (:28), exempts "let's" (:26-27), and still allows "I/my" (:10-12), which contradicts RULEBOOK V1/V2.
- vc--PSQS88VdZU-9 credits White's 5.Nxd4 to Black.
- vc--FRuDd_oXz4-2 and -8 recast the reference coach's own repertoire history as the student's.
- vc--9fPYlSEkJ0-30 says the f4 bishop cannot retreat, but Be3 is legal (chess.js).
- vc--rqPeGKVPbA-8 says "Ng2" for a bishop fianchetto.

7) Other sessions were editing the working tree during this read (learnBoardTeaching.ts, gemCrushLines.ts and reviewFullData.ts were modified). I re-checked the cited lines in those files at the end.

rhythm:
Measured on the 444 notes, which carry 18 videos.

SIZE PER NOTE: median 17 words, mean 22. A quarter (113) are bare move narration with no reason attached.

STUDENT'S MOVES VS THE OPPONENT'S:
- Student moves: median 17-19 words in every phase; 11-22% bare.
- Opponent moves: median 12-15 words; 32-44% bare (endgame opponent moves 44% bare).
- So the student's move almost always gets its job in a clause. The opponent's move is often only named, unless it wants something or costs them something.
- The fat tail is middlegame student moves: mean 33 words, 7 notes of 60+ words.

TWO REGISTERS BY VIDEO TYPE:
- Running blitz commentary: 83-95% of plies get a line, median 11-13 words, a quarter to half of them bare.
- Lesson-style games: only 39-60% of plies get a line, but at 20-25 words. All 57 separate one-line takeaways in the slice sit in the 4 lesson videos (9/13, 17/26, 17/28, 14/20 notes).

OPENING: one clause per move (the move's job, or the opening's name). It swells to about 100 words only at a theory-defining moment: why not the obvious c4; why the bishop that block on d7 is misplaced.

DECISIVE MOMENTS: five notes of 100-545 words (#492 #640 #711 #717 #718), each compressing the rest of a game. They follow a fixed order:
1. status;
2. inventory every threat;
3. the one move that meets them, and why;
4. ready answers to each of their tries;
5. the next step of the plan;
6. king prophylaxis;
7. switch to attack once safe;
8. finishing method (do not give the first check, map the flights);
9. the mating net square by square;
10. a closing generalisation.

ORDINARY BEAT: move → its job (so / which / to) → if the choice is real, the natural alternative and its refutation → the line with their reply in words → the takeaway.

DRAWBACKS: always stated for the opponent's moves. On his own good moves they are stated and then discounted. Weaknesses come with the lever and the piece that will use them.

NUMBERS: only small board counts (attackers, defenders, moves to a crush).

QUESTIONS: never quizzes the student here. He voices THEIR likely question and answers it.

Measured over all 444 notes, counting the words of explains + teaches + plans. One whole game is bare narration, so it is the floor, not the bar.

OVERALL: median 19 words, mean 25. 104 notes (23%) have 8 words or fewer; 25 (6%) have 60 or more.

THE OPPONENT'S MOVES GET LESS: median 15 words, against 21 on the student's own moves. 34% of notes on the opponent's moves are 8 words or fewer, against 16% on the student's own.

BY PLY:
- plies 1-10: median 16
- plies 11-20: median 23. This is the peak: mean 31, and 10 of the 25 long notes fall here
- plies 21-30: median 24
- plies 31-40: median 19
- plies 41 and later: median 15

BY PHASE: opening 20, middlegame 21, endgame 15.

COVERAGE: 232 of the 676 plies inside the videos' spans (34%) have no note. In seven games (videos Qkv7D, QxHsw, RSZf, Rehhb, SDIQ, TNaK, TPTu) nearly every ply has a note: 1-6 plies without one, over spans of 26-52 plies. The other videos have gaps of 5-46 plies, which fits annotating only the key moments (several are lectures). The distiller may also have dropped plies, so a missing note is not proof he was silent.

WHERE THE LONG NOTES FALL:
- the first real decision after book (one game has 9 long notes in a row, 3583-3591);
- the moment the plan is set (3648-3668);
- the justification of a gambit (3578);
- the conversion recipe (3689, 3743);
- the post-mortem.

ORDER INSIDE A LONG NOTE:
1. name the moment;
2. the obvious candidate and what it grabs;
3. the drawback that sets it aside;
4. the pattern the piece configuration suggests;
5. that move against their best reply;
6. a check of what they can do back;
7. the verdict;
8. a return to the shelved candidate if their move changes things.

AFTER A DECISION: the next 3-10 plies drop to one clause or a bare move, the sense of 'the rest is easy'.

QUESTIONS: 14 of 444 notes (3%). Five of them are the obstacle question: what is in the way, and how do you remove it.

THE FLOOR: one whole game (3529-3570, 42 notes) is only the move plus a two-to-four-word function, e.g. 'hitting the queen'.

summary:
Close second reading of public/data/voiced-teachings.json notes[3926..4369]: 444 notes, 37 videos, 247 opening / 149 middlegame / 48 endgame, student Black 264 / White 180. `concepts` is empty on every note and `teaches` is filled on 72, so the teaching sits in `explains`. All code paths below are under /home/user/wt-work and were grep-verified. Any count given as a share is a regex count over this slice, so allow about ±15%.

The coarse reader of this range (Reader 6, notes 3750–4499, 26 acts) missed about 30 distinct acts. The highest-value ones:
1. **A standing opening alarm, heard in 3 different openings:** "your f-pawn has gone, so the queen check on h4 is always in play." No computer reads it. detectKingExposure needs a castled king. detectCentralKingDanger only looks down the king's file.
2. **Reading the board by what is missing:** an absent bishop makes a weakness harmless, or changes what kind of opening it is.
3. **Pin geometry the pin computers don't know:**
   - the would-be pinner is itself pinned;
   - your own quiet pawn move creates a pin on your knight (verified with chess.js);
   - why an offered piece can't be taken (also verified).
   All three are in the family of questions.md item 7.
4. **Piece jobs that change over time:** a job finishes, a job passes to another piece, or a piece is kept because of its job. dutiesOf computes the duty but only feeds chat.
5. **Smaller acts:**
   - castling still allowed by the rules but impossible now, because your piece covers the square;
   - doubled pawns that are not a weakness (tradeQuality.ts:181 scores them as a gain, so today's code teaches the opposite);
   - the "glue" move that re-protects several loose pieces at once;
   - a piece that can't move because every square is forked;
   - the gambit decision menu;
   - analogy to another opening, and where the analogy breaks;
   - in a recap, naming a quiet prophylactic move as the most important move of the game (Review's moment-pickers rank slips only).

**Pattern for F4/F5:** several computers exist for one side only:
- heavyTiedDown and noRetreat run only for the side to move (moveInsight.ts:156);
- speedRunReads runs only on the student's turn (thinkAloud.ts:257);
- theirMoveCost's "bishop shut in" reads only their move;
- blunderCheck reads only the student's blunder.

So the opponent half of each (chase their heavy-piece guard, their piece one pawn push from trapped, their self-block, naming the tunnel vision behind their blunder) is unwired.

**Corrections to the coarse read:**
- A live trapped-piece detector does exist: tacticsDetector.ts:451, run through detectTactics at :833. Only the one-move-ahead version for the opponent is missing.
- mutualPins (speedRunReads.ts:194) exists but uses isPinnedPiece (nextPlans.ts:118), which sees pins against the king only. The slice's case, both knights pinned to queens, never fires.
- The quiet-move-purpose computer is moveIntent.ts:124.

**Do not copy:**
- 35 notes (7.9%) use first person or voice uncertainty.
- One whole 52-note game (#4137–4188) is bare play-by-play.

The name of the reference coach appears nowhere in this output.

I read all 444 notes in voiced-teachings notes[4814..5257] (28 videos) and catalogued 76 distinct teaching acts. Every code claim below was checked by grep with file:line; nothing is from memory. 20 acts are tagged [census missed]: the coarse codes of the 2026-09-27 teaching census do not name them.

Most acts in this slice already have a computer:
- the 26 speed-run reads in speedRunReads.ts, live via thinkAloud.ts:257, called from positionFacts.ts:1009;
- the insight reads in moveInsight.ts, live via positionAsk (:130) and moveMissed (:368);
- the census lanes: moveIntent, moveOrder, recaptureChoice, ruleException, falseAlarm, theirMoveCost, kingAttack, tempoCount, bluffDetector, forkTrick, pinPressure, latentFork, methodBeat.

Three structural gaps dominate.

1. Surface parity (F2, S1).
- computePositionFacts has no Review caller. Its only call sites are CoachTeachPage.tsx:9692, useLiveCoach.ts:264, usePhaseNarration.ts:635, positionReadComposer.ts:130 and whyBestMove.ts:118. So no think-aloud or speed-run read reaches Review.
- Learn-only, all in CoachTeachPage.tsx: moveOrder (:10799), moveIntent (:10837 and :10928), planChoice, trapAhead, openingPlan, rejectedTempting (:9862), opponentIntent (:9558) and opponentGap (:9559).
- moveContrast is Review-only (reviewFullData.ts:938). computePieceOptions is chat-only (coachService.ts:1507).
- The studentMoveTeaching lanes do reach Learn, Review and chat: recapture, ruleException, falseAlarm, kingAttack, trade, kneeJerk, blunderCheck, autopilot, strongChoice. Proof: learnBoardTeaching.ts:138-330, called at coachFeatureService.ts:1970, and through learnBoardTeaching.ts:759 into chat.

2. Dual-use seat gaps (F4).
- latentDanger is only ever called for the student's own seat (positionFacts.ts:488, moveInsight.ts:138). So 'your rook x-rays their king, move the blocker' is never said.
- noRetreat runs only for the student's pieces (moveInsight.ts:287). So 'one pawn move traps their piece' is never said.
- forkTrickFor is voiced only when a quiet move sidesteps it (forkTrick.ts:126-136). It is never voiced as 'available to you but premature' or 'this candidate walks into theirs', even though V19 names this exact trick.

3. Missing computers (new under B6, for David to decide):
- reputation vs reality;
- recapture-names-the-variation and branch-point theory survey (openingBranches.branchesAt exists, no lane speaks it);
- calibrated expectations;
- worst-case firepower test;
- which piece carries out the idea;
- an opponent dilemma where every reply loses something;
- respecting the opponent's resource (today only an LLM instruction at coachPrompts.ts:1225-1231, a G0 smell);
- battery order;
- reassigning a piece whose job is done;
- choosing a rook by the other rook's future job;
- don't kick a piece toward a better square;
- a positional weakness keeps, so there is no rush to punish it;
- opening the position favours the better-developed side (computed today only in Review, not live);
- opposite-coloured bishops as an attacking aim;
- 'bad bishops defend good pawns' and the pawn-colour rule;
- a fortress detector;
- rule exceptions beyond ruleException's three rules;
- the self-question-then-answer device;
- an imagery bank keyed on fact kinds.

Register: 72 of 444 notes are first person and about 4 self-correct. V1 and F18 forbid both, so they must be converted to reasons, not copied.

Side find (G4.5 cap): openingIdentity.ts:144 truncates the computed famous-game list with .slice(0, 2).

Process note: the scratchpad directory is shared across swarm agents. A sibling overwrote my first dump at the generic name slice.txt with other notes (#5258 onward). I discarded it and re-dumped under a unique name, verified to start at #4814 and end at #5257. Swarm agents should use unique scratch filenames.

rhythm:
Measured on this slice (444 notes, 27 videos; 223 opening / 190 middlegame / 31 endgame; 249 notes on the student's move, 195 on the opponent's). Words per note: median 17 (p10 6, p90 42, mean 21); opening 16 (p90 39), middlegame 20 (p90 46), endgame 12 (p90 29). Bands: 1-9 words 30%, 10-24 37%, 25-49 28%, 50-99 5%, plus one 198-word lecture (5911). Own move median 18 words, opponent's move 14.

Two whole videos are bare move narration with no reason anywhere: a Rossolimo (7 notes) and a 2.Bc4-against-the-Sicilian game (55 notes, in which the student loses the queen and a rook with no word on why). They hold 56 of the 134 bare notes. Without them the median is 20 words and 21% of notes are bare, the same share the 1,500-note reader found. Do not copy those videos (V11).

Ply coverage: of 417 consecutive same-video pairs, 74% skip no ply, 19% skip one, 8% skip two or more.

TWO DELIVERY MODES, inversely related, measured per video. Live play-by-play videos comment on 87-100% of plies at a median of 6-24 words. Lecture-style videos comment on 28-45% of plies at a median of 40-48 words: in one quiet Carlsbad game, 13 notes cover 46 plies and 12 of them run 41-69 words. In quiet structural play he does not shorten; he skips the routine ply and spends the words on the next decision. For our coach (V11, never silent) that means a short clause on the routine ply and the full thought at the decision.

Where he goes long (11 notes of 60+ words, none in the endgame):
- the opponent's opening inaccuracy (5748, 84 words: verdict, what it costs them, the structural reason, the sounder set-up, the principle);
- a level position where he has to manufacture chances (5819);
- the conversion recipe after winning material (5863);
- quiet structural decisions (5947-5955);
- one lecture (5911) that folds into a single move a plan, the answer to their tactical try, a recapture rule, a calculation habit, a sacrifice with its conditions, and a mating net.
5 of the 11 sit on the OPPONENT's move: he lays out the student's coming choice while their move is on the board.

Links: 47% of notes over 9 words carry an explicit because / so / but / instead / the point / since / otherwise / rather than / unless / if. 13 notes ask a question and 12 answer it in the same breath; one class-style question is left hanging across two notes (5753-5754). One sentence runs across four plies (5851-5854). The endgame is terse: technique or a result, often one clause.

Measured over notes 7034-7476. These are distilled paraphrases, so absolute word counts run below the raw speech.

PER NOTE. The median is 18 words in the opening (235 notes) and 18 in the middlegame (190). The 18 endgame notes have a median of 22. The 90th percentile is 39 words in the opening, 44 in the middlegame and 39 in the endgame. 93 notes (21%) are 8 words or fewer, bare move calls such as 'Black castles'. 41% of notes follow the OPPONENT's move, so he diagnoses their choice almost as often as he guides the student's.

TWO REGIMES (the key finding). In game-commentary videos he notes 77-100% of the plies, at 12-19 words each, and up to about a third are bare. In lesson videos he notes only 27-47% of the plies, at 37-47 words each, with no bare notes at all. Both spend roughly 10-28 words per ply. The budget is about the same; the distribution is not. Lessons skip the routine plies and pool the words where the idea lives. For the decider, that means pooling the words at decision moments rather than spreading them evenly.

LONG NOTES. The 8 longest notes (60-89 words: 7038, 7096, 7150, 7156, 7161, 7218, 7224, 7282) all sit at a decision point:
- a false alarm with its capture branches;
- an attack window;
- a trap with its conditions;
- a queen post, with their best and their instinctive reply;
- a dead break that becomes a wing plan;
- the wrong ways to break a pin;
- development against material;
- an attack plan with its prerequisites.

ORDER inside a long note: flag the moment, then the condition or why now, then each tempting or popular wrong option with its refutation, then the move and its jobs, then the line or consequence, and the habit or principle last. Opponent-move notes run in a different order: their move plus a verdict, then the board reason, then what it hands you or how to punish it. Their GOOD moves get credit plus the drawback to exploit (7410-7411).

TWO-PLY PATTERN. He states the problem on THEIR move ('their knight now hits e5') and gives the answer on the STUDENT's move ('defend a pawn with a pawn', 7124-7125). That maps to Learn's held move, not to an F05 question.

BY PHASE. The opening carries naming, the opening's ideas as a list, theory status, verdicts on their moves, traps with their conditions and move order. Quiet middlegames carry clamps, plans by destination and prophylaxis. Critical moments carry candidates, the failing tempting move, the line and a safety check (no in-between check). After winning material he consolidates and chooses between attacking and trading. Endgames get short technique and conversion lines.
